{"kind":"assert","text":"Found while implementing this repair, outside its scope, and I am asking rather than taking it.\n\nWHAT I WAS DOING. Your conditions say to reuse the existing environment-sanitization model rather than add a second repository resolver. So I went to find that model, and it is internal/gitstore: storeGitArguments pins \"--no-replace-objects --git-dir \u003crepo\u003e\" on every command, and hermeticGitEnvironment strips GIT_CONFIG and GIT_CONFIG_* and pins the system and global config files to /dev/null. That is a deliberate, documented defence against ambient configuration injection, which is what told me the class is already inside this project's threat model.\n\nWHAT I MEASURED, and this is the part that goes beyond my row. I built two repositories and tested which binding actually stops the redirection.\n\n  --git-dir stops GIT_DIR. With GIT_DIR pointing at the attacker, \"git --git-dir victim/.git config --local --get-regexp ...\" returns the victim's own remote.\n  --git-dir does NOT stop GIT_COMMON_DIR. The same command with GIT_COMMON_DIR pointing at the attacker returns the attacker's remote.\n  Stripping the routing variables from the environment stops both.\n\nSo the fix for my two sites is environment sanitization, exactly as you said, and not an argument change. That part is mine and I am doing it.\n\nTHE PART THAT IS NOT MINE. I then ran the gitstore shape against the same pair — \"git --no-replace-objects --git-dir victim/.git cat-file -p \u003cobject\u003e\", an object that exists only in the attacker repository. Clean, it fails: \"Not a valid object name\". With GIT_COMMON_DIR pointing at the attacker, it prints the attacker's object. The object store is redirected even though --git-dir is pinned. Refs were not redirected in my test — rev-parse of a ref present in both returned the victim's — so what I have established is object reads, not ref resolution.\n\nWHY I THINK IT MATTERS AND WHY I AM NOT SURE HOW MUCH. internal/gitstore is the boundary the kernel verifies through, so an object read that can be pointed at another repository is a different kind of thing from a remote URL being wrong. Against that: anyone who can set your environment variables already has a great deal of local control, and it is legitimate for a project to treat the operator's own environment as trusted. But hermeticGitEnvironment exists because this project already decided ambient injection is in the threat model, and it strips the configuration family while leaving the routing family. That asymmetry looks unintended rather than reasoned, and I would rather ask than assume it was reasoned.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT DOING. Not touching internal/gitstore. It is not among the seven paths, the fix would be a kernel-boundary change, and this row does not authorize it. I am also not filing it as a security defect in the abstract: I have a two-command reproduction and I have stated exactly what it does and does not show.\n\nWHAT I NEED. A ruling on whether this becomes its own request, and if so whether it is mine. Meanwhile I am proceeding with the authorized repair, which strips the routing variables at the internal/app sites this row covers. That fix is correct on its own terms whatever you decide about gitstore."}